  "metadata": {
    "title": "Ark Sees $300 Billion SpaceX Revenue Opportunity by Late 2020s",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.\nArk Investment Management's Brett Winton has outlined a much bigger revenue opportunity for SpaceX as investors look ahead to the company's record IPO pricing. Winton said SpaceX could potentially generate $300 billion in annual revenue by the late 2020s if it deploys tens of gigawatts of orbital data center capacity at today's rental rates. Ark's chief futurist pointed to Starlink, Starship, and SpaceX's launch economics as key parts of the bull case, with SpaceX now the largest position in Ark's venture fund.\nWinton highlighted Starlink as one of the clearest near-term drivers, saying the business could deliver a six-month cash-on-cash return. According to his math, a SpaceX launch filled with satellites costs around $500 million but could produce $1 billion in annual revenue across those units over their five-year lifespan. He also said that moving from Falcon 9 rockets to Starship at the same launch cadence could possibly generate nearly $200 billion in revenue within two years from Starlink satellites alone, without needing the 1,000 annual launches tied to Elon Musk's goal of putting 1 million tons of cargo in orbit within five years.\nArk has projected that SpaceX could reach a $2.5 trillion enterprise value by 2030, though the company reported a $4.28 billion first-quarter loss despite Starlink's profitability. Winton said SpaceX's AI operations do not need to work for investors to achieve strong returns, though profitability could depend on how much capital the company directs toward terrestrial data centers to compete with frontier AI labs. He also said Elon Musk may eventually combine SpaceX with Tesla (NASDAQ:TSLA) once IPO restrictions are lifted, calling that outcome more likely than not and arguing that it could make strategic sense after Musk earlier combined SpaceX and xAI.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "This article first appeared on GuruFocus.",
      "Ark Investment Management's Brett Winton has outlined a much bigger revenue opportunity for SpaceX as investors look ahead to the company's record IPO pricing. Winton said SpaceX could potentially generate $300 billion in annual revenue by the late 2020s if it deploys tens of gigawatts of orbital data center capacity at today's rental rates. Ark's chief futurist pointed to Starlink, Starship, and SpaceX's launch economics as key parts of the bull case, with SpaceX now the largest position in Ark's venture fund.",
      "Winton highlighted Starlink as one of the clearest near-term drivers, saying the business could deliver a six-month cash-on-cash return. According to his math, a SpaceX launch filled with satellites costs around $500 million but could produce $1 billion in annual revenue across those units over their five-year lifespan. He also said that moving from Falcon 9 rockets to Starship at the same launch cadence could possibly generate nearly $200 billion in revenue within two years from Starlink satellites alone, without needing the 1,000 annual launches tied to Elon Musk's goal of putting 1 million tons of cargo in orbit within five years.",
      "Ark has projected that SpaceX could reach a $2.5 trillion enterprise value by 2030, though the company reported a $4.28 billion first-quarter loss despite Starlink's profitability. Winton said SpaceX's AI operations do not need to work for investors to achieve strong returns, though profitability could depend on how much capital the company directs toward terrestrial data centers to compete with frontier AI labs. He also said Elon Musk may eventually combine SpaceX with Tesla (NASDAQ:TSLA) once IPO restrictions are lifted, calling that outcome more likely than not and arguing that it could make strategic sense after Musk earlier combined SpaceX and xAI."
    ],
